NotesString/You can open this notes window for any project by selecting View->Notes...

This example shows data which has been sampled at the outputs of an 
A to D converter in a digital storage oscilloscope. The oscilloscope was 
sampling at 100MHz. The LogicPort sampled the data at 500MHz, yielding 
2ns resolution. This data was acquired in timing mode.

The sampled data is being represented by the LogicPort as an analog 
waveform. Notice that the waveform is "noisy", with spikes appearing 
at various points on the sine wave. What's wrong with this picture? To 
find out, zoom in near any of cursors A through F. You can do this most 
easily by holding down the <Ctrl> key on the keyboard while clicking in 
the area of interest with the mouse. 

You can see that the "noise" is caused by transient data values which 
occur as the ADC's outputs settle at different times. For example, at 
cursor A, the ADC's D7 output goes low 2ns before its other signals go 
high. Though the final value will be 7Eh, the ADC momentarily outputs 
00h as its outputs are settling. This could cause a circuit to malfunction 
in a very unpredictable fashion if some of the ADC's outputs were 
meeting the circuit's setup/hold requirements while others were not. 
The LogicPort's high-speed sampling gives you a window into these 
types of issues.

The "Show Signal Column Only" option has been selected in this example
so that as much data as possible can be displayed. Turn this option on or
off by double-clicking the "Signal" cell of the waveform table.

Timing mode is most useful for measuring the timing relationships between 
signals. From this sampled data, we can determine that the ADC's outputs 
are stable by the falling edge of "ADC Clock". This will be useful information 
when we setup for a state mode acquisition.

To see this same data sampled in state mode, open the file 
"ADC Sine, State mode 100MHz.LPF" . 

Please note that all of the example files are "read only" so that they remain 
intact for future reference. You can change settings in these files for learning 
purposes, but the changes cannot be saved. You can however use the 
"Save As" option to save an editable copy of any example file.
/
